WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.130
- We must mobilize the market.

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.270
- Current commitments are not enough.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.670
- We need public money,
we need private money,

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.810
we need philanthropic money.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.880
- [Narrator] It's no secret
that limiting global warming

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.590
is going to cost a lot of money,

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.207
but just how much is
still a sticking point.

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:20.780
$131 trillion between now
and 2050 is one answer.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.570
According to the International
Renewable Energy Agency,

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.613
much more than previously estimated.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:27.523
For context,

00:00:27.523 --> 00:00:31.810
the world's GDP was nearly
$85 trillion last year.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.980
Of course, it's basically impossible

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.560
to estimate the final cost,

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:36.960
but the world is trying to figure out

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.210
how to meet the 1.5 degrees
Celsius goal set out

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.232
in the Paris Agreement,
amongst other commitments.

00:00:43.232 --> 00:00:47.510
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.440
- The whole economic
system will need to adapt

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.800
and align with the climate objectives.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.530
- [Narrator] It's a huge bill
that would have to be split

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.440
between governments,
businesses, and consumers.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.360
So we broke it down to understand

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.103
where the money needs to go.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.883
First, we'll need to
phase out fossil fuels.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.930
According to this report, $24
trillion in planned investment

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.400
in fossil fuels will need to be redirected

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.810
to energy transition technologies.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.470
- A carbon price and the carbon tax

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.720
is what has been highlighted
as the main instrument

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.620
we should develop to
facilitate the transition.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.590
- [Narrator] Economist Danae Kyriakopoulou

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.650
studies climate and economic development.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.490
She says there are a number of ways

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.820
the world could pay to
phase out fossil fuels.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.250
Although so far, governments
have been reluctant

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:37.780
to raise taxes.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.690
- Subsidies, taxes, direct
investments, regulation,

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.150
all needs to be adjusted to
incentivize this transition.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.410
- [Narrator] Many experts for example,

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.640
say rich countries should
help poor countries

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.720
pay to phase out fossil fuels

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.420
and governments would have to
retrain fossil fuel workers

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.340
for new jobs in clean energy sectors.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.210
Investment in new energy
systems would make up the bulk

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.697
of the $131 trillion price tag.

00:02:02.697 --> 00:02:06.538
$116 trillion would go to
things like efficiency,

00:02:06.538 --> 00:02:09.250
electrification, and new
technologies designed

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.920
to help clean energy solutions
become economically viable.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.920
- This amount is covering
the costs in infrastructure,

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.340
the cost for ensuring
more energy efficiency

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.700
and the deployment of
renewable green hydrogen

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.120
and sustainable biomass.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.820
- [Narrator] But new technologies,

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.640
including sucking carbon
out of the atmosphere

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.300
through carbon capture and
using waste to generate power,

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.930
may actually turn out not to provide

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.890
any large-scale solutions at all.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.560
And estimates for those
ideas like green hydrogen

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.890
to fuel trucks and aircraft
are based on how much

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.563
we think they will one day cost.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.070
Spending this much money
over the next 30 years

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:50.720
would require commitments from the public

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.520
and private sectors.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.780
But there is some debate
about how much money

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.790
each sector should provide.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.960
- Clearly, the private
sector has far more money

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.870
than governments in their ability

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.090
to be able to move on this.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:03.923
- [Narrator] Right now,

00:03:03.923 --> 00:03:06.110
governments are negotiating
investment packages

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.770
and spending and investors
are pouring money

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.133
into sustainable funds.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.580
- Finance has a crucial role
to play in this transition.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.470
It is a very powerful lever
that policymakers have

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:20.530
to direct financial close
away from farming activities

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.150
to activities and sectors
that support this transition.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.050
But while the financial system
is a very powerful lever,

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.410
it is not enough by itself.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.870
- [Narrator] Investors
will also have one eye

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.910
on unpredictable energy prices.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:36.200
Recently, the prices of oil
and natural gas have risen,

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.270
laying bare the world's
continued dependence

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.450
on fossil fuels and the many
worry about the destruction

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.790
of jobs in these industries.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.660
- Even though it is
something that will require

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.940
a lot of investment,
there are returns to this.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.810
There are benefits that will be new jobs,

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:52.910
and it is the growth
story of the 21st century.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:57.030
And if we do not invest, the
cost will be even higher.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.510
- [Narrator] At the
current rate of emissions,

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.670
scientific models predict
the earth will warm

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.800
by a potentially dangerous
2.7 degrees Celsius

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.100
by the end of the century,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.310
compared to pre-industrial levels.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:09.430
Well above the 1.5 degrees Celsius target

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.650
that would limit the
impact of climate change

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.750
and that most countries
have said, they aim to hit.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.880
- We have, today, all the
technologists that we need

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.450
for fighting climate change

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.440
and be consistent with
the Paris Agreement.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.210
The market is already driving
us to a new energy system

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.200
that will be dominated by renewables.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.520
So the hour the English may say

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.615
the direction of travel is clear.

00:04:33.615 --> 00:04:36.115
(light music)

